ible by 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171934 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cb0ae539-5348-5a2c-b226-c009ce6d6094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1045 "Divisibility of Integers by 3, 9, 6, and 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172213 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d1fb6020-a735-5706-80b8-bc04b0d8b879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172603 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6$?\n\n1. $14733189$\n2. $1589610$\n3. $873100584$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7acff681-6951-5e3e-93c7-ed77445de5b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1061 172668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1794b655-2bac-5203-8fdb-02da477fb69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1061 172669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b30e2ba3-941c-5d16-b861-02db07423b35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1061 172672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96c70f43-c735-5619-a8c7-49afb6e904cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1061 172673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46310e8f-1f56-5e58-9836-71090df92551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1061 172676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c02867b-43a4-5116-88da-bc7736bfb7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1061 172677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "777acec7-1e70-587a-80c0-746fd1cdb9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we studied some rules for determining if an integer is divisible by $2,5,10,4,$ and $8.$ In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding of divisibility rules to include $3,9,6,$ and $7.$\n\nWe have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n312\\,561\n$$\n\nIf we sum the digits, we get\n\n$$\n3+1+2+5+6+1 = 18.\n$$\n\nNow, since $18$ is divisible by $3,$ we conclude that $312\\,561$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nThe rule for determining if a number is divisible by $9$ is similar:\n\n*A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$*\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1065 9390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b0f6b89-66c8-5e63-9825-60eb62f1271f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173299 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1064 "The rule for dividing by $7$ is the trickiest rule of the ones we've studied.\n\n*To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ we double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$*\n\nFor example, let's check if the following number is divisible by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n46{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe last digit is ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ If we double this digit, we get $2\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} =  {\\color{blue}{4}}.$ Then, if we subtract ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ from the remaining digits, we get\n\n$$\n46 - {\\color{blue}{4}} = 42.\n$$\n\nNow, since $42$ is divisible by $7,$ we conclude that $462$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nLet's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1065 9391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a5cdfa6e-09a2-5600-9083-a424aaf3d959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173300 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n353,856\n$$\n\nTo check whether this number is divisible by $6,$ we need to check that it's divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3{:}$\n\n- Notice that the last digit of $353,85{\\color{blue}{6}}$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}.$ Since ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is even, $353,856$ is divisible by $2.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- We now check for divisibility by $3.$ The sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+3+8+5+6 = 30.\n$$\n\nSince $30$ is divisible by $3,$ we have that $353,856$ is divisible by $3.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo, since $353,856$ is divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3,$ we conclude that it is divisible by $6.$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1065 9441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "955bbb9e-e7e0-552d-9ecf-ef5efd79239a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173304 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility by 6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying Two Negative Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179821 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8332e831-fb93-567f-ae21-a80716a57f70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442438 #edb/ref 1